          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Years</strong><br>\nGeorges Bizet was born on October 25, 1838, in Paris, France. A musical prodigy, he entered the Paris Conservatoire of Music at the age of nine. Bizet won many awards for his piano and composition work, including the prestigious Prix de Rome in 1857, which provided him the opportunity to study in Italy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Musical Innovations</strong><br>\nBizet was known for his melodic talent and his orchestration skills. He infused his music with dramatic intensity and realism, which was innovative for his time. His compositions often included vibrant characterizations and rich harmonies that were unconventional in the mid-19th century.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Struggles and Reception</strong><br>\nDespite his musical talent, Bizet's works were not immediately successful. His operas were often poorly received by critics and the public, which led to periods of depression and doubt about his career. He supported himself by arranging and transcribing the works of other composers.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
